WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.721 --> 00:00:20.374
Hello, everyone, and welcome to Superstars in History, the talk show where we meet famous people from the

2
00:00:20.494 --> 00:00:26.579
past. Today's guest was the longest-serving first lady of the United States.

3
00:00:27.359 --> 00:00:35.358
While expanding the modern role of the president's wife from hostess to activist, she broke boundaries and encouraged

4
00:00:35.638 --> 00:00:41.661
change. Straight from history, please welcome Eleanor Roosevelt.

5
00:00:58.031 --> 00:00:58.992
Hello, Faye!

6
00:00:59.944 --> 00:01:03.545
It's amazing that you're related to two presidents!

7
00:01:04.626 --> 00:01:12.608
Your uncle Theodore Roosevelt was president in the early 1900s, and your husband, Franklin Delano Roosevelt, also known

8
00:01:12.648 --> 00:01:15.229
as FDR, later became president.

9
00:01:16.349 --> 00:01:23.012
You were born into a wealthy, socially prominent New York family in 1884, but you didn't have the

10
00:01:23.052 --> 00:01:24.192
happiest childhood.

11
00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:32.386
I disappointed my beautiful mother because I was a shy, unattractive child who was afraid of everything.

12
00:01:33.386 --> 00:01:38.550
Before I was ten, both my parents died, so I was raised by my grandmother.

13
00:01:39.511 --> 00:01:45.435
Other children thought I was a strange, sad girl whose dresses were always out of style.

14
00:01:46.336 --> 00:01:51.460
I felt like an outsider and hated that everyone pitied me because I was an orphan.

15
00:01:52.468 --> 00:01:57.112
How did you go from awkward child to activist first lady?

16
00:01:58.253 --> 00:02:04.258
When I was 15, my grandmother sent me to a girls' school near London, where I spent three

17
00:02:04.398 --> 00:02:05.419
happy years.

18
00:02:06.279 --> 00:02:13.145
The headmistress, Mademoiselle Souvestre, encouraged and inspired us to do something with our lives.

19
00:02:14.166 --> 00:02:19.950
I wanted to stay another year, but my grandmother insisted I return to New York City to make

20
00:02:20.030 --> 00:02:21.732
my entry into society.

21
00:02:23.068 --> 00:02:27.009
Oh, I'd love to dress up and go to all those fancy balls!

22
00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:30.630
I hated that!

23
00:02:31.631 --> 00:02:37.552
Thanks to Mademoiselle Souvestre, I knew there was more to life than finding a husband.

24
00:02:38.633 --> 00:02:44.894
I taught classes in the New York City slums and investigated the poor working conditions of women and

25
00:02:44.994 --> 00:02:48.215
children. It sounds like you were busy!

26
00:02:48.576 --> 00:02:49.876
How did you meet your husband?

27
00:02:50.912 --> 00:02:57.375
Franklin was a distant cousin, so we'd met as children before we encountered each other again as young

28
00:02:57.435 --> 00:03:04.538
adults. We married in 1905 and had six children, though one died as an infant.

29
00:03:05.539 --> 00:03:08.600
That was only one crisis you faced together.

30
00:03:09.701 --> 00:03:14.063
In 1921 Franklin contracted polio and was suddenly paralyzed.

31
00:03:15.043 --> 00:03:16.984
He never walked again without assistance.

32
00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:23.964
His mother wanted him to return to his childhood home and become a country gentleman, but I wasn't

33
00:03:24.024 --> 00:03:25.405
going to let that happen.

34
00:03:26.466 --> 00:03:30.008
Before his illness, he'd planned to run for governor of New York State.

35
00:03:31.089 --> 00:03:35.953
I kept his name alive in the Democratic Party by encouraging politicians to visit him.

36
00:03:36.853 --> 00:03:40.816
I also rose to leadership positions in the women's division of the party.

37
00:03:41.817 --> 00:03:46.080
It was a busy time because I was teaching at a girls' school in New York City.

38
00:03:47.228 --> 00:03:53.493
I kept teaching after Franklin was elected governor in 1928, and we had moved to the state capital

39
00:03:53.593 --> 00:03:54.714
several hours away.

40
00:03:54.734 --> 00:03:58.216
I also did some writing and radio work.

41
00:03:59.177 --> 00:04:07.283
When FDR became president in 1933, the Great Depression had left millions of Americans jobless and homeless.

42
00:04:08.284 --> 00:04:15.445
FDR believed the government should take care of people, so he created the New Deal—an ambitious package of

43
00:04:15.525 --> 00:04:17.347
programs to employ and help them.

44
00:04:19.228 --> 00:04:21.190
I'm impressed with how much you know!

45
00:04:22.271 --> 00:04:25.493
Thanks! How did you feel about becoming first lady?

46
00:04:25.513 --> 00:04:33.239
I was reluctant because I dreaded becoming just a hostess, and I'd avoided this role my whole life.

47
00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:39.264
Having earned my own money, I didn't want to give up my work and my financial independence.

48
00:04:40.136 --> 00:04:48.800
Some people criticized me for hosting radio programs, writing magazine articles, and giving speeches—even though I donated my

49
00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:50.100
earnings to charity.

50
00:04:51.221 --> 00:04:56.743
It was unusual back then for married women to work, never mind the first lady.

51
00:04:57.864 --> 00:04:59.784
I always encouraged women to work.

52
00:05:00.585 --> 00:05:08.128
For example, to encourage newspapers to employ women reporters, only women were allowed to attend my press conferences.

53
00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:12.183
You were also well known as a civil rights champion.

54
00:05:12.603 --> 00:05:13.604
How did that start?

55
00:05:14.725 --> 00:05:21.051
As first lady I traveled the country to gather information so I could update Franklin on Americans' opinions

56
00:05:21.132 --> 00:05:21.792
and needs.

57
00:05:22.893 --> 00:05:25.816
I was never afraid to tell him what I thought he should hear.

58
00:05:26.757 --> 00:05:33.703
In 1933, after visiting unemployed coal miners, I pushed for the development of Arthurdale, West Virginia.

59
00:05:34.708 --> 00:05:40.173
This community provided jobs as well as decent housing for miners and their families.

60
00:05:41.254 --> 00:05:46.659
Although many people judged it a failure because of its cost, I was happy that we could help

61
00:05:46.699 --> 00:05:47.659
some families.

62
00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:54.525
But I was disappointed when the first residents banned African Americans from the community.

63
00:05:55.486 --> 00:05:59.910
This led me to become more involved in the fight against racial prejudice.

64
00:06:00.951 --> 00:06:06.254
Speaking of which, tell us about the famous incident with singer Marian Anderson.

65
00:06:07.235 --> 00:06:14.219
In 1939 she wasn't allowed to perform at a Washington, DC, auditorium because she was African American.

66
00:06:15.179 --> 00:06:21.523
I spoke against the organization that owned the venue and helped arrange an outdoor concert at the Lincoln

67
00:06:21.563 --> 00:06:27.907
Memorial that 75,000 people attended—much more than the original venue could hold!

68
00:06:28.907 --> 00:06:30.048
That's incredible!

69
00:06:31.020 --> 00:06:37.402
During World War II, you identified with many mothers because your four sons were serving in the military.

70
00:06:38.402 --> 00:06:43.864
Your visits to American troops in the South Pacific and Great Britain increased your popularity.

71
00:06:44.824 --> 00:06:50.445
Journalists and high-ranking military men were amazed at your energy and stamina.

72
00:06:51.366 --> 00:06:58.127
During my trips I was also working on "My Day," my six-day-a-week newspaper column that I started in

73
00:06:58.147 --> 00:07:05.924
1936. Over the years I wrote about everything from places I visited to issues I wanted to push.

74
00:07:06.865 --> 00:07:12.569
When the British king and queen visited us before the war, I wrote about people criticizing me for

75
00:07:12.649 --> 00:07:13.930
serving hot dogs.

76
00:07:14.971 --> 00:07:17.432
They loved the hot dogs, by the way!

77
00:07:17.452 --> 00:07:27.400
Your years as first lady ended when FDR died unexpectedly in 1945, barely months into his fourth term.

78
00:07:28.548 --> 00:07:32.290
I've heard there were constant rumors you'd run for office.

79
00:07:33.411 --> 00:07:39.474
Although I campaigned for Democratic presidential candidates, I had no interest in running for anything.

80
00:07:40.534 --> 00:07:48.578
From 1945 to 1952, I served as a delegate to the United Nations, where my proudest accomplishment was

81
00:07:48.618 --> 00:07:52.100
the passage of the Universal Declaration of Human Rights.

82
00:07:53.288 --> 00:07:58.312
Those rights ranged from free speech and privacy to health and education.

83
00:07:59.453 --> 00:08:05.438
Wow! You certainly led a full life, but it's time to let you get back to history.

84
00:08:06.459 --> 00:08:07.920
Thank you for having me!

85
00:08:11.803 --> 00:08:15.026
Eleanor Roosevelt continued working with the United Nations.

86
00:08:16.026 --> 00:08:19.417
In the early 1960s, President John F.

87
00:08:19.497 --> 00:08:23.761
Kennedy named her chairperson of his committee on the status of women.

88
00:08:24.781 --> 00:08:29.906
She kept writing, speaking, and traveling the world for causes she believed in.

89
00:08:30.926 --> 00:08:38.032
Her final "My Day" column was published six weeks before she died in 1962 at the age of

90
00:08:38.152 --> 00:08:44.778
78. Today she is remembered as one of the most influential women of the twentieth century.

91
00:08:45.858 --> 00:08:47.520
I hope you enjoyed today's show.

92
00:08:48.196 --> 00:08:51.538
See you next time on Superstars in History.
